WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:03.356 --> 00:07.666 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference July 30,

00:07.666 --> 00:08.726 align:middle
2025 CHAIR POWELL.

00:09.186 --> 00:09.876 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:09.876 --> 00:12.386 align:middle
My colleagues and I
remain squarely focused

00:12.386 --> 00:15.586 align:middle
on achieving our dual-mandate
goals of maximum employment

00:15.756 --> 00:18.806 align:middle
and stable prices for the
benefit of the American people.

00:20.026 --> 00:21.516 align:middle
Despite elevated uncertainty,

00:21.516 --> 00:23.296 align:middle
the economy is in
a solid position.

00:24.456 --> 00:27.746 align:middle
The unemployment rate remains
low, and the labor market is at

00:27.746 --> 00:29.376 align:middle
or near maximum employment.

00:30.556 --> 00:31.836 align:middle
Inflation has been
running somewhat

00:31.836 --> 00:34.406 align:middle
above our 2 percent
longer-run objective.

00:35.086 --> 00:37.816 align:middle
In support of our goals,

00:37.816 --> 00:39.796 align:middle
today the Federal Open
Market Committee decided

00:39.796 --> 00:42.266 align:middle
to leave our policy
interest rate unchanged.

00:43.236 --> 00:44.496 align:middle
We believe that the
current stance

00:44.496 --> 00:47.726 align:middle
of monetary policy leaves us
well positioned to respond

00:47.726 --> 00:50.866 align:middle
in a timely way to potential
economic developments.

00:51.456 --> 00:54.066 align:middle
I will have more to say
about monetary policy

00:54.066 --> 00:56.426 align:middle
after briefly reviewing
economic developments.

00:58.406 --> 01:00.206 align:middle
Recent indicators
suggest that growth

01:00.206 --> 01:02.136 align:middle
of economic activity
has moderated.

01:03.026 --> 01:07.036 align:middle
GDP rose at a 1.2 percent pace
in the first half of this year,

01:07.726 --> 01:09.826 align:middle
down from 2.5 percent last year.

01:11.206 --> 01:13.606 align:middle
Although the increase in the
second quarter was stronger

01:13.606 --> 01:15.756 align:middle
at 3 percent, focusing
on the first half

01:15.756 --> 01:18.586 align:middle
of the year helps smooth
through the volatility

01:18.586 --> 01:20.516 align:middle
in the quarterly figures related

01:20.516 --> 01:23.076 align:middle
to the unusual swings
in net exports.

01:24.576 --> 01:27.306 align:middle
The moderation in growth
largely reflects a slowdown

01:27.306 --> 01:28.316 align:middle
in consumer spending.

01:29.416 --> 01:31.826 align:middle
In contrast, business
investment in equipment

01:31.826 --> 01:34.146 align:middle
and intangibles picked
up from last year's pace.

01:34.776 --> 01:36.996 align:middle
Activity in the housing
sector remains weak.

01:39.046 --> 01:41.446 align:middle
In the labor market,
conditions have remained solid.

01:42.106 --> 01:45.316 align:middle
Payroll job gains
averaged 150,000 per month

01:45.316 --> 01:46.506 align:middle
over the past three months.

01:47.386 --> 01:50.246 align:middle
The unemployment rate, at
4.1 percent, remains low

01:50.296 --> 01:52.926 align:middle
and has stayed in a narrow
range over the past year.

01:54.826 --> 01:55.986 align:middle
Wage growth has continued

01:55.986 --> 01:58.436 align:middle
to moderate while still
outpacing inflation.

01:59.306 --> 02:01.906 align:middle
Overall, a wide set
of indicators suggests

02:01.906 --> 02:05.326 align:middle
that conditions in the labor
market are broadly in balance

02:05.416 --> 02:07.196 align:middle
and consistent with
maximum employment.

02:08.756 --> 02:11.226 align:middle
Inflation has eased
significantly from its highs

02:11.226 --> 02:15.566 align:middle
in mid-2022 but remains
somewhat elevated relative

02:15.566 --> 02:17.346 align:middle
to our 2 percent
longer-run goal.

02:18.616 --> 02:20.826 align:middle
Estimates based on the
consumer price index

02:20.826 --> 02:22.036 align:middle
and other data indicate

02:22.036 --> 02:25.306 align:middle
that total PCE prices
rose 2.5 percent

02:25.306 --> 02:28.106 align:middle
over the 12 months
ending in June and that,

02:28.336 --> 02:30.896 align:middle
excluding the volatile
food and energy categories,

02:31.256 --> 02:33.926 align:middle
core PCE prices rose
2.7 percent.

02:35.296 --> 02:37.216 align:middle
These readings are little
changed from the beginning

02:37.216 --> 02:39.276 align:middle
of the year, although the
underlying composition

02:39.276 --> 02:43.396 align:middle
of price changes has shifted:
Services inflation has continued

02:43.396 --> 02:46.996 align:middle
to ease, while increased
tariffs are pushing up prices

02:46.996 --> 02:48.566 align:middle
in some categories of goods.

02:49.856 --> 02:52.536 align:middle
Near-term measures of inflation
expectations have moved up,

02:52.586 --> 02:55.896 align:middle
on balance, over the course of
this year on news about tariffs,

02:56.316 --> 02:58.196 align:middle
as reflected in both
market-based

02:58.196 --> 02:59.916 align:middle
and survey-based measures.

03:01.056 --> 03:04.106 align:middle
Beyond the next year or
so, however, most measures

03:04.106 --> 03:06.536 align:middle
of longer-term expectations
remain consistent

03:06.536 --> 03:08.266 align:middle
with our 2 percent
inflation goal.

03:10.686 --> 03:13.956 align:middle
Our monetary policy actions
are guided by our dual mandate

03:13.956 --> 03:16.526 align:middle
to promote maximum
employment and stable prices

03:16.526 --> 03:17.526 align:middle
for the American people.

03:17.526 --> 03:20.556 align:middle
At today's meeting,
the Committee decided

03:20.556 --> 03:24.066 align:middle
to maintain the target range for
the federal funds rate at 4¼

03:24.066 --> 03:27.306 align:middle
to 4½ percent and to
continue reducing the size

03:27.306 --> 03:28.006 align:middle
of our balance sheet.

03:29.306 --> 03:31.506 align:middle
We will continue to determine
the appropriate stance

03:31.506 --> 03:33.806 align:middle
of monetary policy based
on the incoming data,

03:34.496 --> 03:37.076 align:middle
the evolving outlook,
and the balance of risks.

03:38.966 --> 03:41.166 align:middle
Changes to government
policies continue to evolve,

03:41.386 --> 03:43.846 align:middle
and their effects on the
economy remain uncertain.

03:45.056 --> 03:47.416 align:middle
Higher tariffs have begun
to show through more clearly

03:47.416 --> 03:50.426 align:middle
to prices of some goods,
but their overall effects

03:50.426 --> 03:53.236 align:middle
on economic activity and
inflation remain to be seen.

03:54.786 --> 03:57.086 align:middle
A reasonable base case
is that the effects

03:57.086 --> 04:00.846 align:middle
on inflation could be short
lived-reflecting a one-time

04:00.846 --> 04:02.036 align:middle
shift in the price level.

04:03.126 --> 04:05.816 align:middle
But it is also possible that
the inflationary effects could

04:05.816 --> 04:08.386 align:middle
instead be more persistent,
and that is a risk

04:08.486 --> 04:10.066 align:middle
to be assessed and managed.

04:11.606 --> 04:14.116 align:middle
Our obligation is to keep
longer-term inflation

04:14.946 --> 04:18.346 align:middle
expectations well anchored and
to prevent a one-time increase

04:18.346 --> 04:21.526 align:middle
in the price level from becoming
an ongoing inflation problem.

04:22.546 --> 04:24.976 align:middle
For the time being, we are
well positioned to learn more

04:24.976 --> 04:27.566 align:middle
about the likely course of the
economy and the evolving balance

04:27.566 --> 04:30.176 align:middle
of risks before adjusting
our policy stance.

04:31.296 --> 04:33.926 align:middle
We see our current policy
stance as appropriate to guard

04:33.926 --> 04:35.136 align:middle
against inflation risks.

04:36.146 --> 04:37.656 align:middle
We are also attentive to risks

04:37.656 --> 04:39.426 align:middle
on the employment
side of our mandate.

04:40.306 --> 04:42.876 align:middle
In coming months, we will
receive a good amount of data

04:43.006 --> 04:45.016 align:middle
that will help inform our
assessment of the balance

04:45.016 --> 04:47.126 align:middle
of risks and the
appropriate setting

04:47.126 --> 04:48.176 align:middle
of the federal funds rate.

04:50.416 --> 04:52.846 align:middle
At this meeting, the Committee
continued its discussions

04:52.846 --> 04:54.986 align:middle
as part of our five-year review

04:54.986 --> 04:56.566 align:middle
of our monetary policy
framework.

04:57.436 --> 04:59.736 align:middle
We focused on potential
revisions to our Statement

04:59.736 --> 05:03.866 align:middle
on Longer-Run Goals and Monetary
Policy Strategy and are on track

05:03.866 --> 05:06.446 align:middle
to wrap up any modifications
by late summer.

05:07.616 --> 05:10.156 align:middle
The Fed has been
assigned two goals

05:10.156 --> 05:12.316 align:middle
for monetary policy-maximum
employment

05:12.486 --> 05:13.976 align:middle
and price-stable prices.

05:14.706 --> 05:16.946 align:middle
We remain committed to
supporting maximum employment,

05:17.166 --> 05:19.646 align:middle
bringing inflation sustainably
to our 2 percent goal,

05:20.246 --> 05:22.666 align:middle
and keeping longer-term
inflation expectations

05:22.666 --> 05:23.206 align:middle
well anchored.

05:24.266 --> 05:25.436 align:middle
Our success in delivering

05:25.436 --> 05:27.576 align:middle
on these goals matters
to all Americans.

05:28.546 --> 05:31.196 align:middle
We understand that our actions
affect communities, families,

05:31.196 --> 05:32.806 align:middle
and businesses across
the country.

05:33.636 --> 05:36.236 align:middle
Everything we do is in
service to our public mission.

05:37.486 --> 05:39.366 align:middle
We at the Fed will
do everything we can

05:39.586 --> 05:41.496 align:middle
to achieve our
maximum-employment

05:41.496 --> 05:42.716 align:middle
and price-stability goals.

05:43.606 --> 05:43.976 align:middle
Thank you.

05:44.146 --> 05:45.196 align:middle
I look forward to
your questions.

05:45.296 --> 05:46.096 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

05:46.506 --> 05:49.406 align:middle
Howard. HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

05:49.406 --> 05:51.056 align:middle
Thanks. Thanks, Chair Powell.

05:51.056 --> 05:53.976 align:middle
There is a lot of "lean" in the
markets and, not to mention,

05:54.036 --> 05:57.396 align:middle
out of the Administration, for
a rate cut now in September.

05:58.306 --> 06:02.386 align:middle
Is that expectation
unrealistic at this point?

06:03.026 --> 06:03.496 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

06:04.346 --> 06:07.796 align:middle
So, as you know, today we
decided to leave our policy rate

06:07.796 --> 06:10.026 align:middle
where it's been, which-where
I would characterize

06:10.026 --> 06:11.346 align:middle
as modestly restrictive.

06:12.086 --> 06:15.576 align:middle
Inflation is running a bit
above 2 percent, as I mentioned,

06:15.776 --> 06:17.296 align:middle
even excluding tariff effects.

06:17.576 --> 06:19.506 align:middle
The labor market's
solid-historically

06:19.506 --> 06:20.316 align:middle
low unemployment.

06:20.906 --> 06:22.426 align:middle
Financial conditions
are accommodative,

06:22.776 --> 06:25.296 align:middle
and the economy is not-the
economy is not performing

06:25.296 --> 06:27.006 align:middle
as though restrictive
policy were holding it

06:27.046 --> 06:28.026 align:middle
back inappropriately.

06:28.766 --> 06:33.596 align:middle
So it seems to, to me and to
almost the whole Committee

06:34.126 --> 06:35.656 align:middle
that the economy
is not performing

06:35.656 --> 06:38.656 align:middle
as though restrictive policy is
holding it back inappropriately,

06:38.656 --> 06:41.456 align:middle
and modestly restrictive
policy seems appropriate.

06:42.276 --> 06:44.086 align:middle
All that said, there's
also downside risk

06:44.136 --> 06:45.506 align:middle
to the labor market.

06:46.056 --> 06:48.846 align:middle
In coming months, we'll
receive a good amount of data

06:49.206 --> 06:51.526 align:middle
that will help inform our
assessment of the balance

06:51.526 --> 06:53.446 align:middle
of risks and the
appropriate setting

06:53.446 --> 06:54.336 align:middle
of the federal funds rate.

06:55.016 --> 06:55.486 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

06:55.486 --> 06:57.216 align:middle
So just to follow up:
By, by "coming months,"

06:57.276 --> 06:59.296 align:middle
does that include the
possibility you'll,

06:59.296 --> 07:02.176 align:middle
you'll be getting
essentially two rounds of jobs

07:02.176 --> 07:05.226 align:middle
and inflation data between
now and the September meeting?

07:06.086 --> 07:08.436 align:middle
Is that potentially
adequate to make a decision

07:08.436 --> 07:09.686 align:middle
to lower rates at that point?

07:09.686 --> 07:10.016 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

07:10.016 --> 07:10.516 align:middle
So you're right.

07:10.566 --> 07:13.516 align:middle
We do have-this is an,
an, an intermeeting period

07:13.516 --> 07:15.716 align:middle
when we'll get two full
rounds of employment

07:15.716 --> 07:18.286 align:middle
and inflation data before the
time of the September meeting.

07:18.596 --> 07:19.646 align:middle
We have made no decisions

07:19.646 --> 07:21.966 align:middle
about September-we don't
do that in advance.

07:21.966 --> 07:24.546 align:middle
We'll be taking that
information into consideration

07:24.546 --> 07:27.656 align:middle
and all the other information
we get as we make our decision

07:27.846 --> 07:28.656 align:middle
at the September meeting.

07:28.876 --> 07:29.786 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

07:30.366 --> 07:33.196 align:middle
Steve. STEVE LIESMAN.

07:33.196 --> 07:35.036 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

07:35.926 --> 07:38.216 align:middle
You took out the
word-or the notion

07:38.216 --> 07:41.126 align:middle
that uncertainty has
diminished from this statement.

07:41.736 --> 07:43.786 align:middle
Does that mean uncertainty
has increased?

07:43.786 --> 07:44.486 align:middle
And I'm just wondering,

07:44.486 --> 07:47.396 align:middle
the Administration has
struck several deals

07:47.396 --> 07:50.526 align:middle
with large trading
partners where it seems

07:50.526 --> 07:53.146 align:middle
like we now know what
the rate is going to be.

07:53.296 --> 07:56.746 align:middle
Does knowing that rate add to
your certainty to change policy,

07:56.746 --> 07:59.796 align:middle
or do you need to wait to
see the economic effects?

08:00.116 --> 08:01.276 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

08:01.716 --> 08:08.876 align:middle
So, essentially, the statement
in the-in the-in our statement

08:08.876 --> 08:11.186 align:middle
about uncertainty
reflects what's gone

08:11.186 --> 08:12.476 align:middle
on since the last meeting.

08:12.476 --> 08:15.756 align:middle
So, at the time of the last
meeting, uncertainty had, had,

08:15.756 --> 08:18.626 align:middle
had moved down a little
bit, but it was more

08:18.626 --> 08:19.746 align:middle
or less even this time.

08:19.746 --> 08:21.926 align:middle
So we took out, you
know, "had diminished"

08:22.166 --> 08:24.536 align:middle
because it didn't
diminish further.

08:24.536 --> 08:25.866 align:middle
So there's not really
much in that.

08:26.346 --> 08:28.466 align:middle
And then your second
question is-say again?

08:29.166 --> 08:29.786 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

08:29.916 --> 08:31.556 align:middle
There have been several
deals that have been struck,

08:31.556 --> 08:33.336 align:middle
and now we seem to have an idea

08:33.336 --> 08:36.686 align:middle
of what the tariff rates
are going to be with some

08:36.686 --> 08:37.956 align:middle
of our large trading partners.

08:37.956 --> 08:40.386 align:middle
Does that not add to the kind
of certainty you might need,

08:40.386 --> 08:42.916 align:middle
or is it you're waiting
for the economic effects

08:43.326 --> 08:44.416 align:middle
to show, show themselves?

08:44.416 --> 08:44.766 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

08:44.766 --> 08:46.966 align:middle
No, I think we're
still-so you're right,

08:46.966 --> 08:50.046 align:middle
it's been a very dynamic time
for these trade negotiations,

08:50.046 --> 08:53.836 align:middle
and lots and lots of events
in the intermeeting period,

08:53.836 --> 08:56.796 align:middle
but we're still, you know,
a ways away from seeing

08:56.796 --> 08:57.746 align:middle
where things settle down.

08:57.996 --> 09:00.236 align:middle
We are clearly getting
more and more information.

09:00.776 --> 09:04.816 align:middle
And, you know, I think at this
point, people's estimates,

09:04.816 --> 09:09.616 align:middle
our estimates, outside estimates
of the-of the likely, you know,

09:09.886 --> 09:13.826 align:middle
effective, effective level
of tariffs is, is not moving

09:13.826 --> 09:15.136 align:middle
around that much at this point.

09:15.186 --> 09:16.466 align:middle
But at the same time,
there are many,

09:16.876 --> 09:18.436 align:middle
many uncertainties
left to resolve.

09:18.616 --> 09:20.376 align:middle
So, yes, we are learning
more and more.

09:20.846 --> 09:23.106 align:middle
It doesn't feel like
we're very close

09:23.106 --> 09:24.226 align:middle
to the end of that process.

09:24.226 --> 09:27.876 align:middle
And that's, that's not for us
to judge, but it does-it feels

09:27.876 --> 09:31.686 align:middle
like there's much more to come,
you know, as well looking ahead.

09:31.686 --> 09:32.116 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

09:33.636 --> 09:34.316 align:middle
Neil. NEIL IRWIN.

09:36.066 --> 09:37.806 align:middle
Hi, Mr. Chairman.

09:38.356 --> 09:39.376 align:middle
Neil Irwin with Axios.

09:39.376 --> 09:41.446 align:middle
This morning, we
got a GDP report

09:41.446 --> 09:44.776 align:middle
in which final domestic private
purchases decelerated-slowest

09:44.776 --> 09:45.706 align:middle
pace since '22.

09:45.706 --> 09:48.586 align:middle
There was a weakness in the
interest-sensitive sectors

09:48.586 --> 09:50.206 align:middle
and residential investment,
commercial structures.

09:50.396 --> 09:51.236 align:middle
Are those not signs

09:51.236 --> 09:53.966 align:middle
that monetary policy is a little
too restrictive right now,

09:54.186 --> 09:55.296 align:middle
given current economic
conditions?

09:55.296 --> 09:55.866 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

09:55.896 --> 10:01.336 align:middle
So the GDP and PDFP numbers
came in pretty much right

10:01.336 --> 10:02.746 align:middle
where we expected
them to come in.

10:02.746 --> 10:04.076 align:middle
You've got to look
at the whole picture.

10:04.076 --> 10:07.346 align:middle
So, certainly, as I mentioned
in my opening remarks,

10:07.786 --> 10:10.166 align:middle
economic activity data, GDP,

10:10.166 --> 10:12.516 align:middle
private domestic final
purchases-which we think is a

10:12.646 --> 10:15.756 align:middle
narrower but better
signal for future,

10:16.176 --> 10:18.656 align:middle
for where the economy
is going-has come

10:18.656 --> 10:21.946 align:middle
down to a little better than 1
percent, 1.2 percent I think,

10:21.946 --> 10:24.146 align:middle
in the case of GDP
for the first half,

10:24.666 --> 10:25.886 align:middle
whereas it was 2½ last year.

10:25.886 --> 10:27.716 align:middle
So that has certainly come down.

10:28.386 --> 10:32.176 align:middle
But if you look at the labor
market, what you see is,

10:32.176 --> 10:33.476 align:middle
by many, many statistics,

10:33.476 --> 10:35.526 align:middle
the labor market is kind
of still in balance.

10:36.546 --> 10:40.256 align:middle
It's things like quits,
you know, job openings,

10:40.256 --> 10:41.966 align:middle
and-let alone the
unemployment rate.

10:42.486 --> 10:45.566 align:middle
They're all very-by many
measures, very similar

10:45.566 --> 10:46.656 align:middle
to where they were a year ago.

10:46.656 --> 10:49.096 align:middle
So you do not see a
weakening in the labor market.

10:49.636 --> 10:51.566 align:middle
You do see a slowing
in job creation,

10:51.566 --> 10:54.566 align:middle
but also in a slowing-a slowing
in the supply of workers.

10:54.566 --> 10:57.166 align:middle
So you've got a labor
market that's in balance,

10:57.506 --> 11:00.976 align:middle
albeit partially because
both demand and supply

11:00.976 --> 11:04.126 align:middle
for workers has-is coming
down at the same pace,

11:04.126 --> 11:06.136 align:middle
and that's why the unemployment
rate has remained roughly,

11:06.626 --> 11:09.696 align:middle
roughly stable, which is why I
said there-we do see downside

11:09.696 --> 11:10.866 align:middle
risk in the labor market.

11:11.126 --> 11:13.926 align:middle
I mean, our two-our two mandate
variables-right?-are inflation

11:14.316 --> 11:15.906 align:middle
and maximum employment-stable
prices

11:15.906 --> 11:17.696 align:middle
and maximum employment,
not so much growth.

11:18.246 --> 11:21.506 align:middle
So the labor market
looks, looks solid.

11:22.066 --> 11:23.826 align:middle
Inflation is above target.

11:23.826 --> 11:27.066 align:middle
And even if you-if you look
through the tariff effects,

11:27.066 --> 11:28.856 align:middle
we think it's still
a bit above target.

11:29.276 --> 11:30.836 align:middle
And that's why our
stance is where it is.

11:30.836 --> 11:33.646 align:middle
But, as I mentioned,
you know, downside risks

11:33.706 --> 11:35.516 align:middle
to the labor market
are certainly apparent.

11:36.096 --> 11:36.476 align:middle
NEIL IRWIN.

11:36.476 --> 11:38.656 align:middle
So on labor, given the fluid
labor supply situation,

11:38.656 --> 11:41.286 align:middle
is there a number for this
jobs report we get on Friday

11:41.286 --> 11:43.876 align:middle
that would look to you like
equilibrium job growth?

11:43.876 --> 11:44.166 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

11:44.166 --> 11:45.706 align:middle
You know, the, the main
number you have to look

11:45.706 --> 11:49.026 align:middle
at now is the unemployment
rate, because if, if-it's true

11:49.026 --> 11:52.056 align:middle
that the, you know, demand
for workers in the form of,

11:52.056 --> 11:53.126 align:middle
let's call it-say-just say,

11:53.126 --> 11:54.926 align:middle
payroll jobs-that
number has come down,

11:54.926 --> 11:57.436 align:middle
but so has the breakeven
number, kind of in tandem.

11:58.006 --> 12:00.336 align:middle
So, you know, as long

12:00.336 --> 12:02.836 align:middle
as the-that puts the labor
market in, in balance.

12:03.176 --> 12:06.446 align:middle
The fact that it's getting
into balance due to declines

12:06.446 --> 12:08.146 align:middle
in both supply and
demand, though,

12:08.146 --> 12:11.756 align:middle
I think does-it is
suggestive of downside risk.

12:11.756 --> 12:14.196 align:middle
So we're-of course, we'll
be watching that carefully.

12:14.886 --> 12:15.366 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

12:15.616 --> 12:17.416 align:middle
Colby. COLBY SMITH.

12:18.926 --> 12:19.316 align:middle
Thank you.

12:19.316 --> 12:22.146 align:middle
Colby Smith with
the New York Times.

12:22.146 --> 12:24.546 align:middle
Two of your colleagues called
for a quarter-point cut today,

12:24.546 --> 12:25.646 align:middle
and I'm wondering what aspects

12:25.646 --> 12:27.986 align:middle
of their argument were
most compelling to you

12:28.236 --> 12:30.166 align:middle
and how you are weighing
their views against those

12:30.566 --> 12:34.996 align:middle
on the Committee who, as of the
June forecast, were in the camp

12:34.996 --> 12:36.756 align:middle
of the Fed holding
interest rates steady

12:36.756 --> 12:38.466 align:middle
for the remainder of the year.

12:38.896 --> 12:42.886 align:middle
And, and just in terms of
the June SEP in particular,

12:43.096 --> 12:44.896 align:middle
is that still the
best representation

12:44.896 --> 12:46.056 align:middle
of where the core
of the Committee is?

12:47.006 --> 12:47.526 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

12:47.526 --> 12:50.046 align:middle
So on, on the dissents, you
know, what you-what you want

12:50.416 --> 12:52.626 align:middle
from everybody and also

12:52.626 --> 12:57.136 align:middle
from a dissenter is a clear
explanation for-of what your

12:57.196 --> 12:58.996 align:middle
thinking is and what are
the arguments you're making.

12:58.996 --> 13:00.046 align:middle
And that's-we had that today.

13:00.046 --> 13:01.206 align:middle
So I think, basically,

13:01.206 --> 13:04.206 align:middle
this was-this was quite a good
meeting all around the table

13:04.236 --> 13:07.346 align:middle
where people were-you know,
thought carefully about this

13:07.346 --> 13:09.606 align:middle
and put their positions
out there.

13:10.096 --> 13:13.516 align:middle
As I mentioned, you know,
the, the-sort of the majority

13:13.516 --> 13:16.836 align:middle
of the Committee was of the
view that inflation's a bit

13:16.836 --> 13:19.816 align:middle
above target, maximum
employment is at target.

13:20.426 --> 13:22.986 align:middle
That calls for modestly
restrictive-in my way

13:22.986 --> 13:24.936 align:middle
of thinking, a modestly
restrictive stance

13:24.936 --> 13:25.996 align:middle
of policy for now.

13:26.806 --> 13:30.026 align:middle
But we had two dissenters
who, I think-you know,

13:30.026 --> 13:33.286 align:middle
you want that clear thinking
and, and, and, you know,

13:34.486 --> 13:37.116 align:middle
expression of your thinking,
and we certainly had that today,

13:37.356 --> 13:38.566 align:middle
I think all around the table.

13:39.196 --> 13:41.776 align:middle
In terms of-you asked
about the June SEP.

13:41.806 --> 13:46.056 align:middle
You know, I wouldn't-you're
right

13:46.116 --> 13:47.666 align:middle
that that's what
it-that's what it says,

13:47.666 --> 13:51.266 align:middle
and that may-that may
well-I, I couldn't point

13:51.266 --> 13:52.656 align:middle
to it six weeks later as,

13:52.806 --> 13:54.626 align:middle
as expressing people's
thought-you really can't

13:54.626 --> 13:54.826 align:middle
do that.

13:54.826 --> 13:57.616 align:middle
We don't run an SEP, and
I don't like to substitute

13:57.836 --> 13:59.776 align:middle
in my own estimate of
what the SEP might be.

13:59.776 --> 14:00.536 align:middle
We don't have one.

14:01.076 --> 14:02.756 align:middle
So I'll just say
that, you know, we,

14:02.756 --> 14:04.946 align:middle
we haven't made any
decisions about September.

14:04.946 --> 14:07.526 align:middle
We'll be monitoring
all the incoming data

14:07.526 --> 14:09.466 align:middle
and asking ourselves whether
the federal funds rate is

14:09.466 --> 14:10.036 align:middle
in the right place.

14:10.036 --> 14:10.606 align:middle
COLBY SMITH.

14:10.606 --> 14:11.416 align:middle
And, just on the point

14:11.416 --> 14:14.776 align:middle
about policy being only modestly
restrictive, does that mean

14:14.776 --> 14:16.296 align:middle
that there's actually
not much scope

14:16.336 --> 14:19.716 align:middle
to reduce rates once the
conditions for a cut are met,

14:20.236 --> 14:22.186 align:middle
barring a significant
weakening of the labor market?

14:22.186 --> 14:22.466 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

14:22.656 --> 14:26.446 align:middle
So let me say, my own estimate
is modestly restrictive.

14:26.586 --> 14:28.346 align:middle
And there are-there
are a range of views

14:28.346 --> 14:29.886 align:middle
of what the neutral rate is

14:29.886 --> 14:32.146 align:middle
at this moment for,
for our economy.

14:32.146 --> 14:34.116 align:middle
And so others may say
it's more restrictive

14:34.116 --> 14:36.116 align:middle
or less restrictive even.

14:36.116 --> 14:42.666 align:middle
You know, we're-we're going to
be-at some point, when we return

14:42.666 --> 14:45.596 align:middle
to moving toward a more
neutral stance, we'll be making

14:45.596 --> 14:46.716 align:middle
that judgment as we go.

14:47.026 --> 14:48.726 align:middle
I don't think we
have a preset course.

14:48.726 --> 14:52.506 align:middle
It's not so mechanical as
saying, you know, "We've derived

14:52.506 --> 14:54.196 align:middle
with great confidence
the neutral rate,

14:54.196 --> 14:56.836 align:middle
and that is our destination,"
because, really, we understand

14:56.836 --> 14:58.626 align:middle
that no one actually knows
what the neutral rate is.

14:59.026 --> 15:00.246 align:middle
We know it by its works,

15:00.886 --> 15:03.446 align:middle
and that will be how-the
way the economy reacts

15:03.516 --> 15:07.736 align:middle
over time to-you know, to
slightly looser policy.

15:07.736 --> 15:08.696 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

15:09.856 --> 15:12.926 align:middle
Nick. NICK TIMIRAOS.

15:14.126 --> 15:15.396 align:middle
Nick Timiraos, the
Wall Street Journal.

15:16.056 --> 15:18.736 align:middle
Chair Powell, my question is
about, what have you learned

15:19.026 --> 15:22.296 align:middle
over the last few months
about the inflation-generating

15:22.346 --> 15:24.166 align:middle
and price pass-through process?

15:24.166 --> 15:27.206 align:middle
And just to drill down: The
June CPI report showed evidence

15:27.206 --> 15:28.886 align:middle
of tariff-induced
goods inflation.

15:29.376 --> 15:33.146 align:middle
Now the tariff landscape is only
starting to be settled with some

15:33.146 --> 15:34.356 align:middle
of these more recent deals.

15:34.486 --> 15:37.216 align:middle
Given the lags between
when tariffs are announced

15:37.606 --> 15:39.336 align:middle
and when they show
up in goods prices,

15:39.986 --> 15:44.096 align:middle
is two months a long enough
horizon to evaluate the impact

15:44.096 --> 15:48.036 align:middle
and be confident that tariffs
aren't impacting the broader

15:48.236 --> 15:49.286 align:middle
inflation process?

15:49.636 --> 15:49.896 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

15:49.896 --> 15:53.116 align:middle
I think you have to think of
this as still quite early days.

15:53.706 --> 15:57.636 align:middle
And so I think what we're seeing
now is substantial amounts

15:57.636 --> 16:00.216 align:middle
of tariff revenue being
collected, on the order

16:00.216 --> 16:02.526 align:middle
of $30 billion a month,
which is, you know,

16:02.526 --> 16:04.756 align:middle
substantially higher
than, than before.

16:05.396 --> 16:10.096 align:middle
And the evidence seems to be,
mostly not paid, but paid only

16:10.096 --> 16:14.506 align:middle
to a small extent, through
exporters lowering their price.

16:15.226 --> 16:18.146 align:middle
And companies or
retailers, sort of people

16:18.146 --> 16:20.016 align:middle
who are upstream-institutions
that are upstream

16:20.016 --> 16:23.396 align:middle
from the consumer, are
paying most of this for now.

16:23.696 --> 16:26.616 align:middle
Consumers are-it's starting
to show up in consumer prices.

16:26.616 --> 16:30.746 align:middle
As you know, in the June report,
we expect to see more of that.

16:30.746 --> 16:33.706 align:middle
And we know from surveys
that companies feel

16:33.706 --> 16:37.626 align:middle
that they have every intention
of, of, of putting this

16:37.626 --> 16:38.446 align:middle
through to the consumer.

16:38.446 --> 16:39.646 align:middle
But, you know, the truth is,

16:39.686 --> 16:41.186 align:middle
they may not be able
to in many cases.

16:41.186 --> 16:43.696 align:middle
So I think it's-we're just
going to have to watch

16:43.696 --> 16:45.666 align:middle
and learn empirically
how much of this

16:45.666 --> 16:47.006 align:middle
and over what period of time.

16:47.716 --> 16:51.036 align:middle
I think we've learned that the
process will probably be slower

16:51.866 --> 16:54.816 align:middle
than, than expected
at the beginning,

16:54.816 --> 16:56.596 align:middle
but we never expected
it to be fast.

16:57.216 --> 17:00.346 align:middle
And we think we have
a long way to go

17:00.346 --> 17:02.676 align:middle
to really understand
exactly how we'll be.

17:02.836 --> 17:04.646 align:middle
So that's how we're
thinking of it right now.

17:04.646 --> 17:05.006 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

17:05.096 --> 17:07.206 align:middle
So if I could follow
up, is the reticence

17:07.206 --> 17:09.466 align:middle
to "look through" core
goods inflation being driven

17:09.466 --> 17:12.346 align:middle
by the judgment that
during the pandemic,

17:12.346 --> 17:14.996 align:middle
expectations proved
more adaptive

17:15.046 --> 17:16.546 align:middle
than anyone at the Fed expected?

17:16.546 --> 17:18.216 align:middle
Is it being driven
by uncertainty

17:18.216 --> 17:19.896 align:middle
over how restrictive policy is?

17:19.896 --> 17:20.416 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

17:20.706 --> 17:22.256 align:middle
You could argue we are, a bit,

17:22.256 --> 17:25.466 align:middle
"looking through" goods
inflation by not raising rates.

17:25.676 --> 17:28.066 align:middle
You know, we haven't
reacted to, to new inflation.

17:28.066 --> 17:30.496 align:middle
But, I mean, I wouldn't-I
wouldn't insist upon that.

17:30.546 --> 17:35.526 align:middle
But I don't think-I think the
base case-I said-as I said,

17:35.626 --> 17:37.156 align:middle
a reasonable base case is

17:37.156 --> 17:41.336 align:middle
that these are one-time-one-time
price effects.

17:42.166 --> 17:44.556 align:middle
Of course, in the end,
there, there will not be.

17:44.556 --> 17:47.156 align:middle
This will not turn
out to be inflation,

17:47.156 --> 17:48.896 align:middle
because we'll make
sure that it's not.

17:48.966 --> 17:51.536 align:middle
We will, through our tools, make
sure that this does not move

17:51.536 --> 17:55.856 align:middle
from being a one-time price
increase to serious inflation.

17:56.336 --> 17:58.436 align:middle
We want to do that efficiently,
though, efficiently.

17:58.436 --> 18:02.646 align:middle
And that means we want to do
it-if you-if you move too soon,

18:03.426 --> 18:07.446 align:middle
you wind up maybe not getting
inflation all the way fixed,

18:07.446 --> 18:08.376 align:middle
and you have to come back.

18:08.376 --> 18:09.166 align:middle
That's inefficient.

18:09.356 --> 18:12.486 align:middle
If you move too late, you
might do too-unnecessary damage

18:12.486 --> 18:13.536 align:middle
to the labor market.

18:14.066 --> 18:18.196 align:middle
So there won't be, in the end,
a big inflationary problem.

18:18.196 --> 18:20.416 align:middle
What we're trying
to do is accomplish

18:20.416 --> 18:22.376 align:middle
that in a way that is efficient.

18:22.846 --> 18:24.816 align:middle
But in the end, there
should be no doubt

18:24.866 --> 18:26.196 align:middle
that we will do what
we need to do

18:26.196 --> 18:27.346 align:middle
to keep inflation under control.

18:27.516 --> 18:28.786 align:middle
Ideally, we do it efficiently.

18:28.786 --> 18:29.136 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

18:29.296 --> 18:33.266 align:middle
Michael. MICHAEL MCKEE.

18:33.926 --> 18:36.316 align:middle
Michael McKee from Bloomberg
Television and Radio.

18:36.736 --> 18:41.926 align:middle
The One Big Bill, leaving aside
the adjectives-do you expect it

18:41.926 --> 18:44.786 align:middle
to add stimulus to
the economy in 2026?

18:44.786 --> 18:47.466 align:middle
And would that be an
argument for remaining on hold

18:47.466 --> 18:48.856 align:middle
or cutting back on the number

18:48.856 --> 18:51.016 align:middle
of rate cuts you would
expect for next year?

18:51.406 --> 18:51.626 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

18:51.876 --> 18:54.536 align:middle
So, of course, let me
just-ritual disclaimer

18:54.536 --> 18:57.006 align:middle
that we don't express any
judgments or anything, right,

18:57.006 --> 18:58.316 align:middle
on, on fiscal legislation

18:58.316 --> 18:59.866 align:middle
or other legislation,
for that matter.

19:00.326 --> 19:03.946 align:middle
But I would say, when you think
that, you know, the biggest part

19:03.946 --> 19:09.156 align:middle
of the bill was, was making
permanent existing law on,

19:09.436 --> 19:14.556 align:middle
on taxes, I don't think we see
it as particularly stimulative.

19:14.626 --> 19:16.196 align:middle
There should be some
stimulative effect,

19:16.726 --> 19:21.046 align:middle
but it shouldn't be significant
over the next couple of years.

19:21.586 --> 19:21.886 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

19:21.916 --> 19:25.596 align:middle
And to follow up, what
do you-well, I don't want

19:25.596 --> 19:27.456 align:middle
to put this in terms of
you and the President,

19:27.456 --> 19:32.906 align:middle
so let me ask it this way: Do
you have concerns about the cost

19:32.906 --> 19:36.576 align:middle
to the government of keeping
rates elevated for longer

19:36.576 --> 19:39.106 align:middle
in terms of interest
rate charges?

19:39.106 --> 19:39.946 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

19:39.946 --> 19:42.516 align:middle
No, that's-you know,
we, we have a mandate,

19:42.516 --> 19:44.536 align:middle
and that's maximum
employment and price stability.

19:45.196 --> 19:51.816 align:middle
And it is-it's not something
we do, to consider the cost

19:51.816 --> 19:54.756 align:middle
to the government
of our rate changes.

19:54.756 --> 19:55.936 align:middle
We have to be able to look

19:55.936 --> 19:59.316 align:middle
at the goal variables Congress
has given us-use the tools they

19:59.316 --> 20:01.796 align:middle
have given us to
achieve those goals.

20:01.926 --> 20:02.806 align:middle
And that's what we do.

20:03.516 --> 20:05.586 align:middle
It's-we don't consider
the fiscal needs

20:05.646 --> 20:06.596 align:middle
of the federal government.

20:06.596 --> 20:08.896 align:middle
No advanced-economy
central bank does that.

20:08.896 --> 20:12.176 align:middle
And it wouldn't be good
for-if we did do that,

20:12.176 --> 20:14.676 align:middle
it would be good neither
for our credibility nor

20:14.676 --> 20:16.516 align:middle
for the credibility
of U.S. fiscal policy.

20:16.516 --> 20:19.236 align:middle
So it's just not something we,
we take into consideration.

20:19.236 --> 20:21.546 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

20:21.546 --> 20:24.476 align:middle
Victoria. VICTORIA GUIDA.

20:25.396 --> 20:28.116 align:middle
Hi. Victoria Guida
with Politico.

20:28.116 --> 20:30.326 align:middle
When it comes to the renovations

20:30.326 --> 20:32.096 align:middle
of the Federal Reserve's
headquarters

20:32.126 --> 20:34.246 align:middle
that the Administration
has been looking into,

20:34.616 --> 20:36.596 align:middle
do you see their
interest in that issue

20:36.596 --> 20:39.186 align:middle
as being directly tied to the
President's push to get you

20:39.186 --> 20:40.096 align:middle
to lower interest rates?

20:41.276 --> 20:41.746 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

20:41.746 --> 20:42.506 align:middle
Not for me to say.

20:42.886 --> 20:46.606 align:middle
I will say, we had a-we had a
nice visit with the President.

20:46.826 --> 20:47.856 align:middle
It was an honor to host him.

20:48.326 --> 20:50.076 align:middle
It's not something
that happens very often

20:50.076 --> 20:52.586 align:middle
at the Federal Reserve-to
have the President come over,

20:52.586 --> 20:55.306 align:middle
let alone to visit a
building-but it was-it was a

20:55.306 --> 20:55.686 align:middle
good visit.

20:55.686 --> 20:56.736 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

20:56.736 --> 20:59.716 align:middle
Are there any aspects of the
project that they've raised

20:59.716 --> 21:02.566 align:middle
that you see as making
you reconsider any aspects

21:02.566 --> 21:03.126 align:middle
of the project?

21:03.656 --> 21:04.376 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

21:04.376 --> 21:07.316 align:middle
So, you know, we-this
project was hatched

21:07.316 --> 21:09.396 align:middle
and conceived almost
a decade ago now,

21:09.396 --> 21:12.876 align:middle
and we went through the very
long process of clearing it

21:12.876 --> 21:14.706 align:middle
through historic preservation

21:14.706 --> 21:18.146 align:middle
at the National Capital
Planning Commission and a lot

21:18.146 --> 21:18.886 align:middle
of back-and-forth there.

21:18.886 --> 21:20.026 align:middle
It was very constructive.

21:20.636 --> 21:21.896 align:middle
We started out to do the work,

21:22.276 --> 21:26.066 align:middle
and we're very well
along on that work.

21:26.416 --> 21:30.506 align:middle
And I was quite pleased to have
the President say multiple times

21:30.506 --> 21:32.086 align:middle
that what he really
wanted to see was,

21:32.846 --> 21:36.476 align:middle
was us getting this construction
completed as soon as possible.

21:36.966 --> 21:39.986 align:middle
That is our focus, and that's
what we're going to do.

21:41.376 --> 21:42.796 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

21:44.616 --> 21:46.966 align:middle
Andrew. ANDREW ACKERMAN.

21:46.966 --> 21:47.766 align:middle
Thanks, Mr. Chairman.

21:47.766 --> 21:50.166 align:middle
Andrew Ackerman with
the Washington Post.

21:50.366 --> 21:51.906 align:middle
What message do you
take from the fact

21:51.906 --> 21:54.486 align:middle
that inflation hit 2.1
percent last September

21:54.796 --> 21:56.456 align:middle
and has bounced higher since?

21:56.796 --> 22:00.306 align:middle
Why do you think financial
conditions are restrictive

22:00.656 --> 22:02.366 align:middle
and in neutral rates
below 4 percent

22:02.416 --> 22:04.566 align:middle
when inflation has stopped
falling for almost a year?

22:05.226 --> 22:05.576 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

22:05.786 --> 22:07.806 align:middle
So inflation-when you talk

22:07.806 --> 22:09.376 align:middle
about these 12-month
inflation measures,

22:09.376 --> 22:12.246 align:middle
you're always battling
residual seasonality.

22:12.706 --> 22:15.726 align:middle
So we'll have, for example,
two months of high inflation,

22:15.726 --> 22:19.666 align:middle
sometimes early in the year,
and then inflation turns lower.

22:19.846 --> 22:22.086 align:middle
And a lot of that may
just be an artifact.

22:22.086 --> 22:23.956 align:middle
So that's why we look
at the 12-month numbers.

22:24.556 --> 22:26.646 align:middle
I-look, I think inflation
is most

22:26.646 --> 22:28.496 align:middle
of the way back to 2 percent.

22:28.866 --> 22:30.966 align:middle
There are things like
the catch-up inflation.

22:30.966 --> 22:33.206 align:middle
So, for example, all
the insurance costs

22:33.516 --> 22:36.586 align:middle
that are now-they're only
now going through inflation,

22:36.586 --> 22:39.006 align:middle
but they actually reflect
inflationary pressures

22:39.006 --> 22:40.446 align:middle
from two, three years ago.

22:40.446 --> 22:42.176 align:middle
So there's-that's
got to go through.

22:42.626 --> 22:45.076 align:middle
In addition, now we have, you
know, three- or four-tenths

22:45.076 --> 22:48.826 align:middle
of inflation in core
inflation from tariffs.

22:49.156 --> 22:52.106 align:middle
So-and we, we, we can't
really separate that out.

22:52.106 --> 22:53.886 align:middle
We're not going to have
a separate, you know,

22:54.026 --> 22:55.596 align:middle
kind of inflation that
isn't the tariffs.

22:55.596 --> 22:57.816 align:middle
We're always going to be dealing
with the whole-all of inflation.

22:57.816 --> 23:00.956 align:middle
But we-the composition, as I
mentioned, has really changed.

23:01.726 --> 23:05.666 align:middle
And, you know, if you go back
to the last couple of years,

23:05.666 --> 23:07.356 align:middle
it was all about
services inflation,

23:07.356 --> 23:08.516 align:middle
which was being very sticky.

23:08.516 --> 23:10.806 align:middle
Now services inflation
is coming down nicely.

23:11.106 --> 23:13.026 align:middle
Goods inflation was
well behaved before,

23:13.196 --> 23:14.796 align:middle
and now goods inflation
is going up.

23:14.796 --> 23:16.346 align:middle
So the story has really changed.

23:16.636 --> 23:18.066 align:middle
That's partially
because of tariffs.

23:18.066 --> 23:21.566 align:middle
It's also partially because we
had restrictive policy in place,

23:21.566 --> 23:24.006 align:middle
and we-and we've
seen that-the result

23:24.006 --> 23:27.546 align:middle
of that gradually work its way
through the services economy.

23:27.546 --> 23:28.626 align:middle
ANDREW ACKERMAN.

23:28.626 --> 23:31.276 align:middle
Okay. The other thing I wanted
to ask is, are you comfortable

23:31.276 --> 23:34.476 align:middle
that BLS can continue performing
their mission effectively

23:34.476 --> 23:36.756 align:middle
if they take an 8 percent
reduction in head count

23:37.056 --> 23:39.296 align:middle
and authorized spending, as the
Administration has proposed?

23:39.296 --> 23:39.816 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

23:40.216 --> 23:42.346 align:middle
I'm not going to comment on
the Administration's proposal.

23:42.526 --> 23:45.486 align:middle
I do think-as I've said, I
think that we-you know, we're,

23:45.486 --> 23:48.256 align:middle
we're getting the data that
we need to do our jobs.

23:48.796 --> 23:51.736 align:middle
And I think it's
really important that,

23:51.896 --> 23:55.846 align:middle
that good data helps not just
the Fed-it helps the government,

23:55.846 --> 23:57.216 align:middle
but it also helps
the private sector.

23:57.286 --> 23:59.656 align:middle
You know, people in
the economy-they,

23:59.826 --> 24:01.526 align:middle
they use this data a lot, too.

24:01.526 --> 24:04.466 align:middle
So it's quite important for
our economy and, certainly,

24:04.466 --> 24:06.806 align:middle
for the Fed's work and other
government agencies' work

24:06.836 --> 24:09.666 align:middle
that we-that we continue
to get better at data.

24:09.666 --> 24:11.396 align:middle
That's what we've been
doing for 100 years.

24:11.396 --> 24:13.126 align:middle
We've been getting better
and better and better.

24:13.256 --> 24:17.486 align:middle
It's very hard to accurately
capture in real time the output

24:17.486 --> 24:20.096 align:middle
of a $20-plus trillion economy.

24:20.596 --> 24:23.256 align:middle
And the United States has been
a leader in that for 100 years,

24:23.256 --> 24:24.966 align:middle
and we really need to
continue that, in my view.

24:25.636 --> 24:26.766 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

24:27.596 --> 24:31.066 align:middle
Edward. EDWARD LAWRENCE.

24:31.066 --> 24:31.756 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

24:31.756 --> 24:33.216 align:middle
Edward Lawrence from
Fox Business.

24:33.536 --> 24:35.636 align:middle
How concerned are you,
with, with the data

24:35.636 --> 24:36.566 align:middle
that we're showing-coming

24:36.566 --> 24:39.476 align:middle
in showing no significant
upward trend in inflation

24:39.476 --> 24:41.486 align:middle
over the past six months,
that the wait-and-see approach

24:41.486 --> 24:45.146 align:middle
for inflation is actually giving
companies cover to raise prices?

24:47.636 --> 24:47.906 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

24:47.906 --> 24:49.606 align:middle
How concerned am I that
the-say that again.

24:49.606 --> 24:50.026 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

24:50.026 --> 24:52.286 align:middle
That the wait-and-see approach
is getting- CHAIR POWELL.

24:52.286 --> 24:53.676 align:middle
The wait-and-see approach-what
do you mean by that?

24:53.676 --> 24:54.006 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

24:54.006 --> 24:54.916 align:middle
For, for cutting rates.

24:55.086 --> 24:58.476 align:middle
You're waiting to see if the
tariffs will affect inflation.

24:59.066 --> 25:00.556 align:middle
So it's a wait-and-see
approach so that- CHAIR POWELL.

25:00.556 --> 25:01.766 align:middle
Well, so that-you
know, that would-that's

25:01.766 --> 25:03.226 align:middle
where we're-policy's
restrictive.

25:03.226 --> 25:05.076 align:middle
When we start cutting,
it'll go toward neutral.

25:05.076 --> 25:06.036 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

25:06.036 --> 25:07.086 align:middle
Okay. This delay, though,

25:07.086 --> 25:09.686 align:middle
where you're saying it's a
one-time-one-time price increase

25:09.936 --> 25:12.206 align:middle
for tariffs, which
they-which possibly could lead

25:12.206 --> 25:14.956 align:middle
into bigger inflation
or more inflation,

25:15.526 --> 25:17.986 align:middle
is that giving companies
cover, though, to raise prices?

25:17.986 --> 25:19.036 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

25:19.036 --> 25:21.386 align:middle
Well, what, what may
be giving-it's not our

25:21.386 --> 25:22.126 align:middle
policy stance.

25:22.126 --> 25:24.956 align:middle
What may be giving-some
companies will certainly be

25:24.956 --> 25:27.256 align:middle
taking advantage of the
fact of the tariffs and all

25:27.256 --> 25:29.616 align:middle
of the discussion of how
they're going to-you know,

25:29.616 --> 25:32.416 align:middle
companies will raise
prices when and as they can.

25:32.976 --> 25:34.486 align:middle
And you-so you saw it famously

25:34.486 --> 25:37.356 align:middle
in the-in the-in the
first Administration

25:37.356 --> 25:40.016 align:middle
of President Trump,
during those tariffs.

25:40.746 --> 25:44.396 align:middle
Washing machines were tariffed,
but, but, but dryers weren't.

25:44.916 --> 25:45.686 align:middle
But what do you know?

25:45.686 --> 25:47.466 align:middle
Price-the price of
dryers went up, too,

25:47.466 --> 25:49.166 align:middle
just like washing machines.

25:49.166 --> 25:52.526 align:middle
So companies will often just
take-they'll cross the street

25:52.526 --> 25:53.976 align:middle
in a group, if you
know what I mean.

25:54.506 --> 25:55.196 align:middle
That'll happen.

25:55.196 --> 25:56.806 align:middle
We don't see a lot of that.

25:56.806 --> 25:59.896 align:middle
I mean, what we see now is
basically the very beginnings

26:00.396 --> 26:03.126 align:middle
of whatever the effects turn
out to be on goods inflation.

26:03.576 --> 26:04.856 align:middle
And, you know, I'll say again,

26:05.346 --> 26:08.666 align:middle
they may be-they may be less
than, than people estimate

26:08.836 --> 26:10.076 align:middle
or more than people estimate.

26:10.356 --> 26:11.276 align:middle
They're not going to be zero.

26:12.006 --> 26:13.956 align:middle
Consumers will pay some of this.

26:14.416 --> 26:15.686 align:middle
Businesses will pay
some of this.

26:15.686 --> 26:17.146 align:middle
Retailers will pay some of this.

26:17.686 --> 26:21.566 align:middle
But, you know, we're just going
to have to, to see it through.

26:21.566 --> 26:22.576 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

26:22.576 --> 26:23.716 align:middle
And just a follow-up,
if I could.

26:23.716 --> 26:26.426 align:middle
Some additional tariffs have
been in place since February,

26:26.746 --> 26:28.146 align:middle
and things, you know,

26:28.146 --> 26:30.146 align:middle
really haven't broken
yet with the economy.

26:30.456 --> 26:32.686 align:middle
So how, how do you justify
to somebody who's looking

26:32.686 --> 26:35.196 align:middle
for a house, who's facing
a 7 percent mortgage

26:35.196 --> 26:36.956 align:middle
and maybe can't afford
those rates?

26:36.956 --> 26:37.796 align:middle
How do you justify that?

26:37.976 --> 26:38.606 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

26:38.976 --> 26:42.956 align:middle
Well, so the housing-the
housing is a-is a special case.

26:42.956 --> 26:48.036 align:middle
Right? Our-we don't set-we
don't set mortgage rates

26:48.036 --> 26:48.616 align:middle
at the Fed, right?

26:48.616 --> 26:51.816 align:middle
We set an overnight rate,
and the rates that go

26:51.816 --> 26:54.066 align:middle
into mortgages are
longer-term rates,

26:54.066 --> 26:55.736 align:middle
like Treasury rates-it
might be 30-year rates,

26:55.736 --> 26:56.636 align:middle
it might be shorter than that,

26:56.636 --> 26:58.076 align:middle
but it's not the
overnight Fed's rate.

26:58.516 --> 27:00.216 align:middle
It's not that we
don't have any effect.

27:00.216 --> 27:02.466 align:middle
We do have an effect, but
we're not the main effect.

27:02.766 --> 27:04.816 align:middle
There are other things
go-though,

27:04.816 --> 27:07.276 align:middle
going on in the housing sector.

27:07.456 --> 27:09.566 align:middle
And one of those is
just, there's kind

27:09.566 --> 27:12.236 align:middle
of a long-term housing
shortage that we have.

27:12.236 --> 27:13.456 align:middle
We haven't built enough housing.

27:13.816 --> 27:15.616 align:middle
This is not something
the Fed can help with,

27:15.706 --> 27:18.736 align:middle
but then that'll be the case
even after things normalize.

27:19.336 --> 27:23.886 align:middle
So I, I think the best thing
that we can do for housing is

27:23.886 --> 27:26.986 align:middle
to have 2 percent inflation
and maximum employment.

27:27.436 --> 27:30.586 align:middle
And that's the-that's what
we can contribute to housing.

27:30.896 --> 27:33.506 align:middle
There are lots of other jobs
to do for the private sector

27:33.506 --> 27:35.856 align:middle
and Congress, but that's
what we're trying to get to.

27:35.856 --> 27:37.416 align:middle
And, I mean, we've made a
lot of progress toward that.

27:37.416 --> 27:39.446 align:middle
We're in-we have a very
good labor market right now.

27:39.896 --> 27:42.216 align:middle
Inflation-we were very
close to 2 percent.

27:42.216 --> 27:45.186 align:middle
We're seeing some goods
inflation move us away but,

27:45.256 --> 27:46.446 align:middle
so far, not very far away.

27:48.446 --> 27:48.736 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

27:48.786 --> 27:52.426 align:middle
Chris. CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

27:52.426 --> 27:53.236 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

27:53.236 --> 27:53.606 align:middle
Thank you.

27:53.976 --> 27:55.376 align:middle
Well, can you give us
a little more about,

27:55.376 --> 27:57.806 align:middle
what kind of economic
data does the Fed need

27:57.806 --> 28:00.056 align:middle
to see before you'll
be ready to cut?

28:00.056 --> 28:03.396 align:middle
I mean, do you need inflation
back nearly to target?

28:03.996 --> 28:06.926 align:middle
Are there other things in the
pricing that you look for?

28:07.176 --> 28:09.056 align:middle
Do you need to see
weakening in the job market?

28:09.316 --> 28:10.916 align:middle
What kind of things
are you looking for?

28:11.176 --> 28:11.956 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

28:11.956 --> 28:16.586 align:middle
I mean, ultimately, it's-it
could be any-it could be any

28:16.906 --> 28:17.946 align:middle
of those things.

28:17.946 --> 28:21.716 align:middle
Right? But, but, you know,
if you saw that the risks

28:21.716 --> 28:25.016 align:middle
to the two goals were moving
into balance, if they were fully

28:25.016 --> 28:26.306 align:middle
in balance, that would imply

28:26.306 --> 28:29.536 align:middle
that you should move toward
a more-a more neutral stance

28:29.536 --> 28:29.996 align:middle
of policy.

28:29.996 --> 28:32.646 align:middle
This is-this is the special
situation we're in, which is,

28:32.646 --> 28:35.266 align:middle
we have two-sided risk,
risk to both of our goals.

28:36.136 --> 28:41.696 align:middle
When we paused, inflation
was above target,

28:41.696 --> 28:43.326 align:middle
and the labor market
was pretty good.

28:43.326 --> 28:45.856 align:middle
So, so, you know,
that was a time

28:45.856 --> 28:48.916 align:middle
when policy-policy was
restrictive when we paused.

28:48.916 --> 28:52.896 align:middle
And to be restrictive is to
be-to be supporting a return

28:52.896 --> 28:54.416 align:middle
to our inflation target, right?

28:54.936 --> 28:57.266 align:middle
So as the two targets
get back into balance,

28:57.596 --> 29:01.196 align:middle
you would-you would think you'd
move in, in, in a way closer

29:01.196 --> 29:03.406 align:middle
to neutral and that
the next-the next steps

29:03.406 --> 29:05.296 align:middle
that we take are likely
to be in that direction.

29:05.606 --> 29:06.586 align:middle
What will it take?

29:06.666 --> 29:09.946 align:middle
You know, it'll just take-it'll
be the totality of the evidence.

29:09.946 --> 29:12.556 align:middle
As I mentioned, there's quite
a lot of data coming in, which,

29:12.636 --> 29:16.126 align:middle
before the next meeting, will
it be dispositive of that?

29:16.366 --> 29:17.636 align:middle
I-you know, it's
really hard to say.

29:17.636 --> 29:19.766 align:middle
We don't make those
decisions right now.

29:19.826 --> 29:21.306 align:middle
So we'll have to see.

29:21.306 --> 29:21.836 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

29:22.456 --> 29:24.726 align:middle
Well, I guess just in terms of
inflation, though, for example,

29:24.726 --> 29:28.396 align:middle
like, will, will you-some
people would point to,

29:28.396 --> 29:30.826 align:middle
if it remains only in
goods and it doesn't bleed

29:30.826 --> 29:33.106 align:middle
over to services, then
maybe that's evidence

29:33.626 --> 29:34.956 align:middle
that the tariff effect is going

29:34.956 --> 29:36.566 align:middle
to be a temporary,
one-time thing.

29:36.566 --> 29:38.866 align:middle
Is that kind of thing
affect your thinking,

29:38.866 --> 29:41.296 align:middle
or do you just need to see the
number come down-closer to 2?

29:41.296 --> 29:41.656 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

29:41.656 --> 29:43.516 align:middle
I think-we'll, we'll,
we'll look at everything.

29:43.566 --> 29:47.486 align:middle
You know, it's-as I
mentioned, you know,

29:47.486 --> 29:49.866 align:middle
a pretty reasonable base case is

29:50.556 --> 29:52.576 align:middle
that this will be a
one-time price increase.

29:53.146 --> 29:55.626 align:middle
And, in the end, we'll make
sure that that's the case.

29:55.626 --> 29:57.486 align:middle
We're just trying to
do that efficiently.

29:57.486 --> 30:00.546 align:middle
We're-and "efficiently" means
getting the timing right

30:00.546 --> 30:03.986 align:middle
so we don't-again, if we
go-if we cut rates too soon,

30:04.426 --> 30:06.386 align:middle
maybe we didn't finish
the job with inflation.

30:06.436 --> 30:08.526 align:middle
There's-history is dotted
with examples of that.

30:08.926 --> 30:11.896 align:middle
If you cut too late, then maybe
you're doing unnecessary damage

30:11.896 --> 30:12.616 align:middle
to the labor market.

30:12.616 --> 30:15.176 align:middle
So we're trying to-we're trying
to get that timing right,

30:15.816 --> 30:17.716 align:middle
and that's effectively
what we're doing.

30:19.716 --> 30:21.516 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

30:22.336 --> 30:23.426 align:middle
Claire. CLAIRE JONES.

30:23.426 --> 30:24.946 align:middle
Claire Jones, Financial Times.

30:25.456 --> 30:27.076 align:middle
Just a question on the dollar.

30:27.206 --> 30:30.446 align:middle
We've seen it decline
quite heavily this year.

30:30.446 --> 30:33.136 align:middle
I was wondering if there's
been any discussion about that

30:33.136 --> 30:35.616 align:middle
at the meetings and
how-to what degree

30:35.616 --> 30:37.476 align:middle
that might be complicating
your attempts

30:37.546 --> 30:39.656 align:middle
to get inflation back to target.

30:39.656 --> 30:40.306 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

30:40.306 --> 30:42.466 align:middle
So this goes back to
the division of labor

30:42.466 --> 30:44.726 align:middle
between the Fed and the
Treasury-as you, I'm sure,

30:44.726 --> 30:49.906 align:middle
know-and, you know, the,
the, the Treasury only speaks

30:49.946 --> 30:51.266 align:middle
to the-speaks to the dollar.

30:51.266 --> 30:56.046 align:middle
It's not-it's not something
that's been a topic of, of,

30:57.396 --> 31:01.266 align:middle
you know, major discussion at
all at, at-at the-at the Fed.

31:01.266 --> 31:02.486 align:middle
I won't say it doesn't come up.

31:02.696 --> 31:04.326 align:middle
The [FOMC meeting]
transcripts, when they come

31:04.326 --> 31:06.286 align:middle
out in a few years-they'll
probably reflect some mentions

31:06.286 --> 31:06.676 align:middle
of the dollar.

31:06.676 --> 31:08.646 align:middle
But it would never
be a, a major focus.

31:08.686 --> 31:09.036 align:middle
CLAIRE JONES.

31:09.536 --> 31:12.126 align:middle
And just to follow on, if I
may, to Andrew's question,

31:12.176 --> 31:15.286 align:middle
I think the amount
of imputed data

31:15.286 --> 31:17.756 align:middle
in CPI now is up to 35 percent.

31:17.756 --> 31:20.026 align:middle
I mean, is there any
discussion of that as well?

31:20.026 --> 31:21.246 align:middle
Is there any consideration

31:21.246 --> 31:24.276 align:middle
of looking at-into alternative
measures, data scraping

31:24.276 --> 31:28.026 align:middle
and so on, in order to just
ensure you've got a good read

31:28.026 --> 31:30.326 align:middle
on what's happening to
prices in the U.S. economy?

31:30.746 --> 31:31.046 align:middle
Thank you.

31:31.046 --> 31:31.516 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

31:31.516 --> 31:33.806 align:middle
Yeah, we're-so we're
monitoring the situation.

31:33.806 --> 31:36.666 align:middle
We do, of course-I mean, as
you know, during the pandemic,

31:36.666 --> 31:39.756 align:middle
we looked at a whole lot
of new kinds of data.

31:40.776 --> 31:42.986 align:middle
People are looking at big
data sets that you can get

31:43.096 --> 31:45.026 align:middle
from all sorts of places.

31:45.026 --> 31:46.646 align:middle
And we do all of that.

31:46.786 --> 31:49.766 align:middle
But at the same-we really
need-the government data really

31:49.766 --> 31:53.966 align:middle
is the gold standard in data,
and we need it to be-you know,

31:53.966 --> 31:56.546 align:middle
to be good and be
able to rely on it.

31:56.926 --> 32:00.776 align:middle
And we're not going to be
able to substitute for that.

32:00.976 --> 32:03.256 align:middle
But if-we have to make
do with what we have,

32:03.256 --> 32:06.046 align:middle
but I certainly hope
that we get what we need.

32:08.516 --> 32:08.856 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

32:09.066 --> 32:09.436 align:middle
Jay O'Brien.

32:09.776 --> 32:10.206 align:middle
JAY O'BRIEN.

32:12.276 --> 32:12.986 align:middle
Hi, Mr. Chairman.

32:12.986 --> 32:14.386 align:middle
Jay O'Brien, ABC News.

32:14.436 --> 32:18.146 align:middle
President Trump has obviously
invoked your name a lot.

32:18.146 --> 32:19.996 align:middle
He has personally pressured you.

32:20.446 --> 32:22.356 align:middle
Are you concerned the way

32:22.356 --> 32:25.526 align:middle
that conduct might impact
the Fed's independence

32:25.526 --> 32:26.256 align:middle
going forward?

32:26.606 --> 32:27.176 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

32:27.546 --> 32:30.416 align:middle
I'll just say that-so I, I think

32:30.456 --> 32:33.456 align:middle
that having an independent
central bank has been an

32:33.456 --> 32:37.286 align:middle
institutional arrangement that
has served the public well.

32:37.786 --> 32:39.596 align:middle
And as long as it
serves the public well,

32:40.666 --> 32:42.286 align:middle
it should continue
and be respected.

32:42.286 --> 32:44.626 align:middle
If it didn't serve
the public well, then,

32:45.396 --> 32:46.406 align:middle
then it wouldn't be something

32:46.406 --> 32:48.146 align:middle
that we should just
automatically defend.

32:48.146 --> 32:51.286 align:middle
But what it gives us and other
central banks-what it gives you

32:51.286 --> 32:53.296 align:middle
is the ability to make these,

32:53.486 --> 32:57.686 align:middle
these very challenging decisions
that-in ways that are focused

32:57.686 --> 33:00.586 align:middle
on the data and the evolving
outlook, the balance of risks,

33:00.586 --> 33:01.776 align:middle
and all the things
we talk about,

33:02.246 --> 33:03.606 align:middle
and not political factors.

33:03.606 --> 33:06.926 align:middle
And so governments all over
the advanced-economy world have

33:06.926 --> 33:09.756 align:middle
chosen to put a little
bit of distance

33:09.976 --> 33:12.786 align:middle
between direct political
control of those decisions

33:13.576 --> 33:14.556 align:middle
and the decisionmakers.

33:14.556 --> 33:17.936 align:middle
So if you-if you were-if
you weren't-if you were not

33:17.936 --> 33:20.966 align:middle
to have that, that would be a
great temptation, of course, to,

33:21.416 --> 33:25.216 align:middle
to use interest rates to
affect elections, for example.

33:25.936 --> 33:28.206 align:middle
And that's something that,
that we don't want to do.

33:28.206 --> 33:30.316 align:middle
So I think that's
pretty widely understood.

33:30.316 --> 33:31.576 align:middle
Certainly, it is in Congress.

33:32.186 --> 33:35.346 align:middle
And, I mean-I mean, I
think it's very important.

33:35.346 --> 33:35.936 align:middle
I'll just say that.

33:36.576 --> 33:38.146 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

33:38.146 --> 33:38.766 align:middle
Maria Eloisa.

33:43.396 --> 33:44.816 align:middle
MARIA ELOISA CAPURRO.

33:44.816 --> 33:45.676 align:middle
Afternoon, Chair Powell.

33:45.746 --> 33:47.696 align:middle
Maria Eloisa Capurro
from Bloomberg News.

33:48.146 --> 33:51.516 align:middle
You mentioned a slowdown in
consumer spending, and I wanted

33:51.586 --> 33:54.456 align:middle
to see if you could "walk us
through" what was the discussion

33:55.316 --> 33:56.786 align:middle
with the Committee around that.

33:56.826 --> 33:59.346 align:middle
We've seen delinquency
rates rising

33:59.346 --> 34:01.056 align:middle
for upper-income households.

34:01.406 --> 34:04.426 align:middle
How do you see that evolving
in the next few months,

34:04.426 --> 34:07.076 align:middle
and how much of a
vulnerability that is

34:07.076 --> 34:08.596 align:middle
for the economy going forward?

34:09.116 --> 34:09.726 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

34:10.526 --> 34:13.876 align:middle
Consumer spending had been very,
very strong for the last couple

34:13.876 --> 34:19.236 align:middle
of years and had-repeatedly,
forecasters, not just us,

34:19.316 --> 34:21.286 align:middle
had been forecasting
it would slow down.

34:22.026 --> 34:23.546 align:middle
And now maybe it finally has.

34:23.736 --> 34:30.636 align:middle
So I would say, you know, if you
talk to credit card companies,

34:30.636 --> 34:32.856 align:middle
for example, they will tell
you that the consumer is

34:32.856 --> 34:36.216 align:middle
in solid shape and that
spending is at a healthy level.

34:36.216 --> 34:38.806 align:middle
It's not growing rapidly,
but it's at a healthy level.

34:39.166 --> 34:41.326 align:middle
And delinquencies are
not, not a problem.

34:41.326 --> 34:43.136 align:middle
You mentioned high-end
delinquencies.

34:43.136 --> 34:44.376 align:middle
I don't know what
to make of that.

34:44.376 --> 34:45.236 align:middle
I read the same thing.

34:45.236 --> 34:48.386 align:middle
But-so, generally-and
if you look at the banks

34:48.386 --> 34:51.176 align:middle
and when the banks talk about
in their earnings calls,

34:51.686 --> 34:53.936 align:middle
their-the performance
of credit has been good.

34:54.306 --> 34:57.156 align:middle
So, essentially, you, you have
a consumer that's in good shape

34:57.156 --> 34:58.986 align:middle
and is spending,
not at a rapid rate.

34:59.706 --> 35:02.546 align:middle
But it, it, it's true.

35:02.546 --> 35:05.016 align:middle
And it was, again, right in
line with what we expected,

35:05.856 --> 35:08.896 align:middle
the GDP data that
we got this week.

35:10.276 --> 35:12.626 align:middle
So-and I think it's still
a little bit difficult

35:12.626 --> 35:15.736 align:middle
to interpret, because you
have these massive swings

35:15.736 --> 35:20.066 align:middle
in net exports, which, which
may also be affecting-you know,

35:20.166 --> 35:23.036 align:middle
some of that can be affected
by-can affect consumer spending

35:23.036 --> 35:23.296 align:middle
as well.

35:23.586 --> 35:25.296 align:middle
Look, we-it's one
of the data points

35:25.296 --> 35:27.266 align:middle
that we pay most
careful attention to,

35:28.026 --> 35:30.266 align:middle
and there's no question
that it's slowed.

35:31.086 --> 35:32.556 align:middle
And, you know, we're
watching it closely.

35:32.556 --> 35:33.996 align:middle
But we also watch
the labor market

35:33.996 --> 35:35.046 align:middle
and the performance
of inflation.

35:35.046 --> 35:36.896 align:middle
Those are our two-those
are our two variables

35:36.896 --> 35:38.816 align:middle
that we're assigned to maximize.

35:38.816 --> 35:39.596 align:middle
MARIA ELOISA CAPURRO.

35:39.596 --> 35:41.806 align:middle
And just to follow up on what
my colleagues were asking

35:41.806 --> 35:44.486 align:middle
about the dissents:
Governor Waller said

35:44.486 --> 35:47.856 align:middle
that the labor market is on
edge, and he was pointing

35:47.856 --> 35:49.966 align:middle
to weaknesses on
the private sector.

35:50.346 --> 35:52.666 align:middle
I was wondering, you've said
that the main number to look

35:52.666 --> 35:54.736 align:middle
at is the unemployment
number overall,

35:54.826 --> 35:57.136 align:middle
but what was the
discussion about the state

35:57.136 --> 35:58.566 align:middle
of the private jobs market?

35:58.566 --> 35:59.876 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

36:00.166 --> 36:03.566 align:middle
So I'm not going to talk about
any individual's-you know,

36:03.626 --> 36:04.706 align:middle
any individual's comments.

36:04.706 --> 36:05.416 align:middle
I wouldn't do that.

36:05.416 --> 36:07.956 align:middle
But, look, I think
what we know is

36:08.836 --> 36:12.736 align:middle
that private-sector
job creation, you know,

36:12.796 --> 36:15.156 align:middle
certainly in the
last report-we'll see

36:15.156 --> 36:18.016 align:middle
on Friday-but had
come down a bit.

36:18.166 --> 36:25.786 align:middle
And, and if you-if you take
the QCEW adjustment seriously,

36:25.786 --> 36:28.286 align:middle
with it-it may be even
low-maybe close to zero.

36:28.966 --> 36:33.576 align:middle
But the unemployment rate
is still-was still low.

36:33.956 --> 36:36.476 align:middle
So, what that's telling you
is that, that, you know,

36:36.596 --> 36:38.926 align:middle
demand for workers is
slowing, but so is the supply.

36:39.086 --> 36:42.186 align:middle
So that's a-it's in
balance, oddly enough.

36:42.266 --> 36:44.986 align:middle
You've got a very low
unemployment rate, and it's kind

36:44.986 --> 36:49.116 align:middle
of been there for a year as
job creation has moved down,

36:49.116 --> 36:51.066 align:middle
but also we know that, you know,

36:51.066 --> 36:52.726 align:middle
because of immigration
policy really,

36:53.246 --> 36:57.066 align:middle
the flow into our labor forces
is just a great deal slower.

36:57.066 --> 37:00.016 align:middle
And those two things have
slowed more or less in tandem.

37:00.226 --> 37:02.376 align:middle
If you look at things,
like I mentioned, quits,

37:02.806 --> 37:07.826 align:middle
look at wages-wages are
gradually cooling-look

37:08.086 --> 37:10.326 align:middle
at vacancies to unemployment,

37:10.326 --> 37:12.916 align:middle
those things have been pretty
stable for-they haven't really

37:12.916 --> 37:14.366 align:middle
moved a lot in a full year.

37:14.826 --> 37:16.586 align:middle
So I think if you
take the totality

37:16.586 --> 37:19.256 align:middle
of the labor market data,
you've got a solid labor market.

37:19.716 --> 37:22.066 align:middle
But I think you have to see
that there are downside risks.

37:22.316 --> 37:24.846 align:middle
It's not-you don't see
weakening in the labor market,

37:25.436 --> 37:28.186 align:middle
but I think you've got
downside risks in a world

37:28.636 --> 37:30.696 align:middle
where unemployment's being
held down because both demand

37:30.696 --> 37:31.826 align:middle
and supply are declining.

37:31.826 --> 37:34.556 align:middle
I think that's-it's worth
paying close attention

37:34.556 --> 37:35.346 align:middle
to it, and we are.

37:37.016 --> 37:37.316 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

37:37.316 --> 37:40.566 align:middle
Nancy. NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

37:41.306 --> 37:42.796 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

37:42.796 --> 37:45.126 align:middle
Nancy Marshall-Genzer
with Marketplace.

37:45.566 --> 37:49.786 align:middle
One more question on
the lack of unanimity

37:49.786 --> 37:51.566 align:middle
in today's decision,
the two dissents.

37:51.986 --> 37:53.236 align:middle
Was there talk during
the meeting?

37:53.236 --> 37:54.286 align:middle
I know you're not going to talk

37:54.286 --> 37:56.696 align:middle
about what-exactly what
individuals said, but,

37:56.886 --> 37:58.736 align:middle
in general, was there
talk during the meeting

37:58.736 --> 38:00.656 align:middle
of cutting rates,
and what was the case

38:00.656 --> 38:02.236 align:middle
against that at the meeting?

38:02.616 --> 38:02.826 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

38:02.946 --> 38:06.036 align:middle
Sure. So, you know, we have-we
have an economic go-round,

38:06.036 --> 38:07.476 align:middle
where people talk
about the economy,

38:07.476 --> 38:09.416 align:middle
and then the next-to-then
to-that's yesterday.

38:09.416 --> 38:11.556 align:middle
And then today, we have a
monetary policy go-round,

38:11.556 --> 38:13.626 align:middle
all the way around the
table-everyone gives

38:13.626 --> 38:14.166 align:middle
their views.

38:14.166 --> 38:20.386 align:middle
So the discussion around policy
was-the majority view was,

38:20.656 --> 38:22.166 align:middle
was still what it has been,

38:22.166 --> 38:24.146 align:middle
which is that inflation
is running above target,

38:24.496 --> 38:26.606 align:middle
maximum employment
is right at target.

38:26.956 --> 38:29.976 align:middle
That means policy, policy should
be a little bit restrictive,

38:30.036 --> 38:34.306 align:middle
somewhat restrictive, because
we want-we want inflation to,

38:34.396 --> 38:35.896 align:middle
to move all the way
back to its target.

38:36.176 --> 38:38.496 align:middle
So that's where people
have been and still are.

38:39.126 --> 38:43.466 align:middle
Two of our members felt that
the time had come to cut

38:43.686 --> 38:45.356 align:middle
and that they-for the reasons

38:45.356 --> 38:46.486 align:middle
that they're-they're
going to express.

38:46.486 --> 38:47.816 align:middle
I won't tell you the reasons.

38:47.866 --> 38:51.036 align:middle
They-they'll issue some
kind of a-of a thing

38:51.036 --> 38:51.986 align:middle
in the next day or so.

38:52.416 --> 38:54.956 align:middle
So-but that's the story.

38:54.996 --> 38:57.576 align:middle
And I would say, you
know, well argued,

38:57.576 --> 38:59.076 align:middle
very thoughtfully argued all

38:59.076 --> 39:00.776 align:middle
around the table,
good arguments.

39:00.856 --> 39:06.256 align:middle
And, you know, it's a situation
where-an unusual situation.

39:06.256 --> 39:08.456 align:middle
The economy is in-is in,
you know, good shape,

39:08.666 --> 39:12.176 align:middle
but it's an unusual
situation where you have risks

39:12.176 --> 39:14.446 align:middle
to both your employment
mandate and your inflation.

39:14.526 --> 39:16.226 align:middle
That's the nature
of a supply shock.

39:16.696 --> 39:20.926 align:middle
And it's probably not surprising
that there would be differences

39:21.016 --> 39:23.936 align:middle
and different perspectives on
that as well as different views

39:23.936 --> 39:27.116 align:middle
of where the neutral rate
is, so they-different views

39:27.116 --> 39:28.566 align:middle
of how tight policy is.

39:28.566 --> 39:30.056 align:middle
So we have those.

39:30.166 --> 39:32.876 align:middle
I will say, what you hope
is that people, you know,

39:32.876 --> 39:36.616 align:middle
explain their, their positions
very thoughtfully and clearly.

39:36.616 --> 39:39.476 align:middle
And we absolutely had that today
all the way around the table.

39:39.476 --> 39:40.366 align:middle
I would call it one

39:40.366 --> 39:43.436 align:middle
of the better meetings I can
recall from that standpoint.

39:43.626 --> 39:44.496 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

39:44.496 --> 39:45.666 align:middle
And you've said you're waiting

39:45.666 --> 39:48.836 align:middle
to be confident inflation is
heading toward your 2 percent

39:48.836 --> 39:50.676 align:middle
target before you
start cutting rates.

39:50.826 --> 39:54.366 align:middle
When you do get that confidence,
would you be in-would you be

39:54.366 --> 39:56.866 align:middle
in favor of lowering
rates right away?

39:57.996 --> 39:58.366 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

39:58.366 --> 40:00.446 align:middle
It's not, not quite
the way I would put it.

40:00.756 --> 40:02.966 align:middle
You know, I said that's
why we think policy should,

40:03.046 --> 40:05.586 align:middle
should be restrictive,
is because, you know,

40:06.376 --> 40:07.686 align:middle
inflation is above target.

40:08.386 --> 40:14.396 align:middle
When we-when we have
risks to both goals-one

40:14.396 --> 40:16.086 align:middle
of them is farther
away from goal

40:16.086 --> 40:17.476 align:middle
than the other, and
that's inflation.

40:17.536 --> 40:18.796 align:middle
Maximum employment's at goal.

40:18.796 --> 40:20.846 align:middle
So you have to-that means
policy should be tight

40:20.846 --> 40:22.976 align:middle
because tight policy is
what brings inflation down.

40:23.676 --> 40:26.916 align:middle
If you came to the view that
the risks to the two were more

40:26.916 --> 40:28.756 align:middle
in balance, that would imply

40:28.756 --> 40:30.086 align:middle
that policy shouldn't
be restrictive,

40:30.086 --> 40:33.106 align:middle
it should be more
neutral-more, more,

40:33.506 --> 40:34.616 align:middle
you know, a neutral stance.

40:34.616 --> 40:36.676 align:middle
And that would be somewhat
lower than where we are now.

40:36.676 --> 40:38.136 align:middle
No one knows exactly
where that is.

40:38.706 --> 40:41.926 align:middle
So that's-that's the framework
I think I'd be taking.

40:42.156 --> 40:45.846 align:middle
And, you know, we'll
just have to see.

40:45.846 --> 40:47.586 align:middle
We're going to be
obviously looking at a lot

40:47.586 --> 40:49.356 align:middle
of data in the next cycle.

40:49.356 --> 40:52.016 align:middle
It is one of the cycles
where we have two employment

40:52.016 --> 40:55.346 align:middle
and two inflation reports, and
we'll see where that takes us.

40:55.706 --> 40:56.666 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

40:57.106 --> 41:02.026 align:middle
Jeff Cox. JEFF COX.

41:02.026 --> 41:03.456 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman,
for taking the question.

41:03.456 --> 41:05.686 align:middle
Jeff Cox from CNBC.com.

41:05.686 --> 41:08.156 align:middle
A metric that you like to
cite a lot is the final sales

41:08.156 --> 41:09.786 align:middle
to private domestic purchasers.

41:09.786 --> 41:14.846 align:middle
That was down to a 1.2 percent
gain in the second quarter

41:14.916 --> 41:17.066 align:middle
from 1.9 percent in Q1,

41:17.066 --> 41:20.336 align:middle
suggesting that there's some
softening in underlying demand.

41:20.916 --> 41:23.086 align:middle
Just wondering if you look
at that and you combine

41:23.086 --> 41:25.636 align:middle
that with some of the housing
numbers that-with the weakness

41:25.636 --> 41:27.006 align:middle
that you acknowledge at the top

41:27.006 --> 41:30.326 align:middle
of your remarks-that the housing
market is, in fact, weak.

41:30.496 --> 41:35.176 align:middle
And the inflation numbers from
GDP today came down 2.1 percent

41:35.176 --> 41:37.046 align:middle
for headline, 2.5
percent for core.

41:37.346 --> 41:40.206 align:middle
I'm wondering how much more
movement you would need to see

41:40.486 --> 41:44.276 align:middle
from these data points before
you would be comfortable

41:44.276 --> 41:45.906 align:middle
with cutting in, say, September.

41:46.986 --> 41:47.776 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

41:47.776 --> 41:51.516 align:middle
It's going to be the total-hard
to answer that specifically.

41:51.516 --> 41:54.666 align:middle
PDFP, I think, for the first
half-private domestic final

41:54.666 --> 41:56.696 align:middle
purchases, or final sales,

41:56.696 --> 41:59.976 align:middle
as people call it-was
1.6 on the first half.

42:00.166 --> 42:02.316 align:middle
GDP, I believe, was 1.2 percent.

42:02.766 --> 42:03.696 align:middle
So that's the whole half.

42:03.696 --> 42:04.816 align:middle
You mentioned the quarters.

42:05.406 --> 42:06.616 align:middle
So those are slower.

42:06.766 --> 42:10.916 align:middle
But, you know, GDP is
bumpy quarter to quarter,

42:10.916 --> 42:13.496 align:middle
half to half, and
often gets revised,

42:13.656 --> 42:14.806 align:middle
you know, after the fact.

42:15.496 --> 42:17.896 align:middle
The labor market data,
we, we still think,

42:17.896 --> 42:19.576 align:middle
is-continue to think,

42:19.576 --> 42:21.686 align:middle
is the best data we
have on the economy.

42:21.686 --> 42:24.266 align:middle
And that shows a 4.1
percent unemployment rate.

42:24.566 --> 42:28.696 align:middle
It shows wages, you know,
still at a healthy level,

42:28.696 --> 42:31.766 align:middle
but moving ever closer
to what we would regard

42:31.766 --> 42:32.996 align:middle
as long-run sustainable,

42:32.996 --> 42:34.836 align:middle
consistent with longer-run
productivity

42:34.836 --> 42:35.866 align:middle
and 2 percent inflation.

42:35.866 --> 42:38.536 align:middle
So the labor market is
actually still quite solid.

42:39.206 --> 42:41.486 align:middle
Inflation is above target.

42:41.486 --> 42:44.596 align:middle
Even ignoring tariffs, it's
a little bit above target.

42:44.596 --> 42:48.856 align:middle
And tariffs-so we're watching
all of that and, again,

42:48.856 --> 42:53.696 align:middle
trying to-trying to
do the right thing

42:53.696 --> 42:55.446 align:middle
in what is a challenging
situation,

42:55.446 --> 42:58.916 align:middle
because you're being pulled
in two directions and you have

42:58.916 --> 43:02.026 align:middle
to decide which of
those it took to go in.

43:02.136 --> 43:04.976 align:middle
And, actually, at some point,
if they're-if they're sort

43:04.976 --> 43:06.576 align:middle
of equally at risk, then
you really want to be

43:06.576 --> 43:08.736 align:middle
at a neutral policy stance,
which we're not right now.

43:08.816 --> 43:11.606 align:middle
JEFF COX. So it'd be safe to say
that, if the data kind of stays

43:11.606 --> 43:12.756 align:middle
in line with where
it is right now,

43:12.756 --> 43:15.076 align:middle
that you wouldn't be comfortable
with cutting in September?

43:15.076 --> 43:15.486 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

43:15.486 --> 43:18.806 align:middle
I'm not going to-I'm
not going to say that.

43:18.806 --> 43:21.146 align:middle
No. I, I, I just think we're
going to need to see the data,

43:21.956 --> 43:26.296 align:middle
and it can go in many different
directions-the inflation data

43:26.296 --> 43:30.036 align:middle
and, and the employment data.

43:30.206 --> 43:32.476 align:middle
And we'll just-we're going to
make a judgment based on all

43:32.476 --> 43:35.136 align:middle
of the data and based on that
balance-of-risks analysis

43:35.166 --> 43:35.676 align:middle
that I mentioned.

43:35.766 --> 43:36.266 align:middle
JEFF COX. Thank you.

43:36.896 --> 43:38.176 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

43:39.386 --> 43:43.526 align:middle
Greg Robb, for the
last question.

43:43.526 --> 43:43.886 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

43:43.886 --> 43:44.316 align:middle
Thanks, Chair Powell.

43:44.316 --> 43:45.486 align:middle
Greg Robb from MarketWatch.

43:46.166 --> 43:48.726 align:middle
The Treasury Secretary
has said recently

43:48.726 --> 43:52.396 align:middle
that it would be confusing
for the markets if you stayed

43:52.396 --> 43:56.146 align:middle
on as a Governor after your
term on the-as Chair ends.

43:56.146 --> 43:58.566 align:middle
And I was wondering
if you had any update

43:58.566 --> 44:00.796 align:middle
for us on-a decision
on that front.

44:00.976 --> 44:01.186 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

44:01.186 --> 44:03.046 align:middle
Sorry, I do not have
any update for you.

44:03.816 --> 44:04.736 align:middle
Thanks very much, everyone.

